## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses understanding of bridging, threading, backpressure management, and lifecycle handling in a practical logging scenario.

## Key Concepts
- React Native bridge patterns (DirectEvent vs EventEmitter)
- Background thread file tailing with rotation detection
- Fixed-size buffering and batch emission to preserve ordering guarantees
- Backpressure strategies: dropping oldest versus dropping newest
- App lifecycle handling for background task management

## Usage Snippet
```javascript
const Logs = require('react-native-logs');

const logs = Logs.startLogStream({
  logFilePath: '/var/log/app.log',
  pollIntervalMs: 500,
  maxEntriesPerBatch: 100,
});

logs.onLog((entries) => {
  console.log('New log entries:', entries);
});

// Later, when done:
logs.stopLogStream();
```

## Trade-offs
| Strategy | Trade-off |
|----------|------------|
| Drop oldest | Lower memory floor; risk losing historical context |
| Drop newest | Preserve context; may miss recent critical errors |
| File rotation by inode | Reliable on most filesystems; requires platform-specific handling |
| File rotation by size | Simpler to implement; less robust |
| Pause on background | Lower power usage; may miss logs during suspension |
| Background task | Continuous logging; higher battery consumption |

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your understanding of on-device machine learning integration, multi-threading in React Native, and JSI/TurboModule bridge design for low-latency computer vision tasks.

## Key Concepts
- On-device inference pipelines for real-time object detection
- JSI/TurboModules for high-performance native-JavaScript communication
- Multi-threading models with dedicated worker queues
- Frame pacing, memory management, and energy optimization on mobile devices

## Native API Sketch (JSI/TurboModule)
```cpp
// ObjectDetectorModule.h (C++ TurboModule)
class ObjectDetectorModule : public JavaScriptModule {
public:
  void startDetection(FrameSource source);
  void stopDetection();
  void setModel(ModelConfig config);
  void onDetectionResult(DetectionCallback callback);
};
```

## Thread Model
- **Main Thread**: Handles React Native bridge communication and callback dispatching
- **Camera Thread**: Dedicated thread capturing frames from camera feed at 30 FPS
- **Inference Thread**: Worker thread running TensorFlow Lite/CoreML for neural network execution
- **Post-Processing Thread**: Applies NMS and formats results

## Minimal Usage Snippet
```javascript
import { ObjectDetectorModule } from 'react-native-object-detector';

const detector = new ObjectDetectorModule({
  modelPath: 'models/yolov8n.tflite',
  numThreads: 4,
  confidenceThreshold: 0.5,
  nmsThreshold: 0.4,
});

detector.onResults((detections) => {
  console.log('Detected objects:', detections);
});

await detector.start({ source: 'camera', fps: 30 });
```

## Critical Considerations

### Frame Pacing
- Use a triple-buffer scheme to decouple camera capture from inference processing
- Implement a frame drop strategy when inference latency exceeds frame interval (33ms at 30 FPS)
- Timestamp each frame to maintain temporal ordering in the output stream

### Memory Management
- Pre-allocate tensor buffers to avoid dynamic allocation during inference
- Use object pooling for detection result objects to minimize GC pressure
- Release camera frames immediately after tensor conversion to prevent memory buildup

### Energy Considerations
- Run inference on the dedicated NPU/DSP when available (delegate to EdgeTPU, Neural Engine)
- Throttle to lower FPS (15) when the app is backgrounded or screen is off
- Batch results instead of emitting each detection individually to reduce bridge overhead

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a simple, production-like RN screen with network fetch, list virtualization, refresh, pagination, and responsive layout.

## Key Concepts

- FlatList usage, keyExtractor, getItemLayout
- RefreshControl, onRefresh, onEndReached
- Pagination, error handling, retries
- Orientation responsiveness, 2-column layout
- Performance, memory management

## Code Example

```javascript
import React, {useEffect, useState, useCallback} from 'react';
import {FlatList, View, Text, Image, RefreshControl} from 'react-native';

export default function UsersList(){ 
  const [data,setData] = useState([]);
  const [loading,setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [refreshing,setRefreshing] = useState(false);
  const [page,setPage] = useState(1);
  const [error,setError] = useState(null);

  const fetchUsers = async (pageNum=1, refresh=false) => {
     try {
        if (refresh) setRefreshing(true);
        else setLoading(true);
        const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/users?page=${pageNum}`);
        const json = await res.json();
        setData(prev => refresh ? json : [...prev, ...json]);
        setError(null);
     } catch (e) { setError(e); }
     finally { setLoading(false); setRefreshing(false); }
  };

  useEffect(()=>{ fetchUsers(); },[]);

  const onRefresh = () => fetchUsers(1,true);
  const onEndReached = () => { fetchUsers(page+1); setPage(p=>p+1); };

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={data}
      keyExtractor={item => String(item.id)}
      numColumns={2}
      renderItem={({item}) => (
        <View style={{flex:1, padding:8}}>
          <Image source={{uri:item.avatar}} style={{width:60,height:60,borderRadius:30}}/>
          <Text>{item.name}</Text>
        </View>
      )}
      refreshControl={<RefreshControl refreshing={refreshing} onRefresh={onRefresh} />}
      onEndReached={onEndReached}
      onEndReachedThreshold={0.5}
      ListEmptyComponent={<Text>{error ? "Error loading" : "No data"}</Text>}
    />
  );
}
```

